-- Reviewed corrections from the supplied 2026-10-26 export. Historical papers remain unchanged.
UPDATE question_bank SET question_text='Pointing to his own childhood photograph, Suresh says, "This boy is the only son of my mother." Who is the boy?',options='[{"id": "a", "text": "Suresh himself"}, {"id": "b", "text": "His brother"}, {"id": "c", "text": "His uncle"}, {"id": "d", "text": "His cousin"}]'::jsonb,explanation='Suresh is his mother’s only son. The boy in his childhood photograph is Suresh himself, not his son.' WHERE id='b89c9708-5798-49e6-8252-cabaf563c1f3' AND question_text IS NOT DISTINCT FROM 'Pointing to a photograph of a boy, Suresh said, "He is the only son of my mother." How is Suresh related to that boy?' AND options IS NOT DISTINCT FROM '[{"id": "a", "text": "Father"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Brother"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Uncle"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Cousin"}]'::jsonb AND explanation IS NOT DISTINCT FROM '"The only son of my mother" refers to Suresh himself (assuming Suresh is a male speaker). Since the boy in the photo is his son, Suresh is the boy’s father.';
UPDATE question_bank SET question_text='For a twice-differentiable function near an interior point c, which conditions are individually sufficient to guarantee a strict local maximum at c?',options='[{"id": "a", "text": "f′(c) = 0 alone"}, {"id": "b", "text": "f′(c) = 0 and f″(c) < 0"}, {"id": "c", "text": "f′(c) = 0 and f″(c) > 0"}, {"id": "d", "text": "f′ is positive immediately to the left of c and negative immediately to the right"}]'::jsonb,correct_option_ids='["b", "d"]'::jsonb,explanation='Both b (second derivative test) and d (first derivative sign test) suffice. A stationary point alone can be a minimum or neither. Option c gives a strict local minimum.' WHERE id='b105e973-2097-4b8b-b377-912cc7731b7c' AND question_text IS NOT DISTINCT FROM 'For a twice-differentiable function f(x), which conditions guarantee that x = c is a strict local maximum?' AND options IS NOT DISTINCT FROM '[{"id": "a", "text": "f’(c) = 0"}, {"id": "b", "text": "f’’(c) < 0"}, {"id": "c", "text": "f’’(c) > 0"}, {"id": "d", "text": "f’(x) changes sign from positive to negative as x increases through c"}]'::jsonb AND correct_option_ids IS NOT DISTINCT FROM '["a", "b", "d"]'::jsonb AND explanation IS NOT DISTINCT FROM 'By the second derivative test, f’(c) = 0 and f’’(c) < 0 indicates concave down, giving a local maximum (a, b). By the first derivative test, f’ switching from + to - also confirms a local maximum (d). Option (c) describes a local minimum.';
UPDATE question_bank SET question_text='Find the smallest positive integer greater than 12 that leaves remainder 2 when divided by 6, 8 and 12.' WHERE id='ebab91a2-9a92-4046-9013-0387025edeb0' AND question_text IS NOT DISTINCT FROM 'Find the smallest number which when divided by 6, 8, and 12 leaves a remainder of 2 in each case.';
UPDATE question_bank SET question_text='Five persons P, Q, R, S, T sit in a row facing North. S sits immediately between T and Q. Q is to the immediate left of R. P is to the immediate left of T. Which of the following statements are correct?' WHERE id='5ee2d8c5-fd3e-4705-b775-e7a1e05c1528' AND question_text IS NOT DISTINCT FROM 'Five persons P, Q, R, S, T sit in a row facing North. S is between T and Q. Q is to the immediate left of R. P is to the immediate left of T. Which of the following statements are correct?';
UPDATE question_bank SET question_text='Assume at least one rose exists. Statements: 1. All roses are flowers. 2. Some flowers are red. Conclusions: I. Some roses are red. II. Some flowers are roses. Which conclusions logically follow?' WHERE id='ce0276c1-3f11-44ef-8529-e507010027fa' AND question_text IS NOT DISTINCT FROM 'Statements: 1. All roses are flowers. 2. Some flowers are red. Conclusions: I. Some roses are red. II. Some flowers are roses. Which conclusions logically follow?';
UPDATE question_bank SET question_text='Suppose a/b = c/d, where a, b, c and d are nonzero real numbers and a ≠ b and c ≠ d. Which of the following relations necessarily hold?' WHERE id='c56351cb-ef3a-4239-8064-90b01ab4ca4b' AND question_text IS NOT DISTINCT FROM 'For four numbers a, b, c, d in continued proportion (a/b = c/d), which of the following algebraic relations are mathematically true?';
UPDATE question_bank SET question_text='Using the excess-kurtosis convention, a normal distribution has excess kurtosis zero.',explanation='Excess kurtosis subtracts 3 from the standardized fourth central moment. The normal distribution has ordinary kurtosis 3 and excess kurtosis 0. A kurtosis value alone does not determine peak shape.' WHERE id='c881b27d-f3c4-4e50-9add-8bfdb1c636cd' AND question_text IS NOT DISTINCT FROM 'A distribution with positive kurtosis (leptokurtic) has heavier tails and a sharper central peak than a standard normal distribution.' AND explanation IS NOT DISTINCT FROM 'True. Excess kurtosis > 0 indicates heavier tails and higher peak density than the Gaussian bell curve (mesokurtic, excess kurtosis = 0).';
UPDATE question_bank SET correct_answer_text='ROC curves plot recall against false-positive rate. With a rare positive class, even a small false-positive rate can yield many false alerts relative to true positives. A high ROC-AUC therefore need not imply useful precision. Class imbalance alone does not force a high ROC-AUC. Inspect the precision-recall curve, average precision, class prevalence and the operating threshold alongside ROC-AUC. Average precision and trapezoidal PR-AUC are distinct summaries.',explanation='ROC-AUC describes ranking discrimination. Precision-recall analysis adds information about positive predictions under class imbalance; imbalance alone does not guarantee any particular AUC.' WHERE id='887151bc-338d-488e-8ca8-e5746b7d025b' AND correct_answer_text IS NOT DISTINCT FROM 'In severely imbalanced datasets (e.g. 99.9% negative class, 0.1% positive class in fraud detection), the False Positive Rate (FPR = FP / (FP + TN)) has an enormous True Negative denominator. Even if thousands of false alarms occur (large FP), FPR remains small because TN dominates the denominator. This keeps the ROC curve close to the top-left corner, giving an inflated ROC-AUC (>0.95). In such cases, the Precision-Recall curve (PR-AUC / Average Precision) should be used instead: Precision directly compares true positives against false positives without including true negatives in the calculation.' AND explanation IS NOT DISTINCT FROM 'Large TN denominator masks high false positive counts in ROC-AUC; PR-AUC isolates minority class performance.';
UPDATE question_bank SET correct_answer_text='DataFrame.apply normally applies a function to each column with axis=0, or to each row with axis=1. Built-in array operations can avoid repeated Python callbacks and often improve numeric workloads. Performance depends on dtype, operation, engine, data size and pandas version. Benchmark the actual workload rather than assuming a fixed speedup.',explanation='The default axis is column-wise. Numeric vectorization can reduce Python callback overhead, but neither contiguous memory nor a fixed speedup is guaranteed.' WHERE id='d3937d85-aee2-40be-9dbf-30bbed75ce90' AND correct_answer_text IS NOT DISTINCT FROM 'Vectorized NumPy operations (such as `df["A"] + df["B"]` or `np.log(df["A"])`) execute in compiled C code over contiguous memory blocks with SIMD hardware optimization, operating at near C-speeds. In contrast, `df.apply(func)` iterates row-by-row in Python user-space, invoking a Python function call frame for every single record and incurring substantial interpreter overhead. Vectorized operations are frequently 50× to 300× faster than `.apply()`.' AND explanation IS NOT DISTINCT FROM 'Vectorization operates directly on compiled memory; .apply() introduces per-row Python interpreter loop overhead.';
UPDATE question_bank SET question_text='For independent identically distributed observations with finite mean and finite nonzero variance, which distribution does the standardized sample mean approach as sample size increases?',explanation='The classical iid CLT gives sqrt(n)(sample mean − μ)/σ converging in distribution to a standard normal. There is no universal sample-size cutoff of 30.' WHERE id='9ac13adc-5703-45b7-9e9b-8035f9d23afd' AND question_text IS NOT DISTINCT FROM 'According to the Central Limit Theorem (CLT), as sample size n increases (typically n ≥ 30), what distribution does the sample mean approach?' AND explanation IS NOT DISTINCT FROM 'The CLT states that the sampling distribution of the sample mean approaches a normal distribution N(μ, σ²/n) regardless of the underlying population distribution shape, provided variance is finite.';
UPDATE question_bank SET question_text='Compare a list comprehension with an equivalent append-based loop in CPython. Are memory or speed advantages guaranteed?',correct_answer_text='Both construct a list and store its elements. Comprehensions can reduce interpreter overhead and make simple transformations clearer, but their speed depends on Python version and workload. They still evaluate Python expressions. Neither a fixed percentage speedup nor a universal memory saving is guaranteed. A generator expression can avoid constructing the entire result list.',explanation='Comprehensions build lists; generators yield values lazily. Compare readability and benchmark performance on the target interpreter.' WHERE id='620ff629-4b5f-4389-9317-36bae530f274' AND question_text IS NOT DISTINCT FROM 'Explain how list comprehensions provide memory and execution speed advantages over manual `for` loops in standard CPython.' AND correct_answer_text IS NOT DISTINCT FROM 'List comprehensions execute in CPython at optimized C-level speed: the bytecode uses specialized instructions (`LIST_APPEND`) without the overhead of repeatedly looking up and calling the `.append()` method on the list object in the Python virtual machine loop frame. While both allocate the resulting list in memory, list comprehensions avoid the function call overhead of method resolution per iteration, typically resulting in 20–35% faster execution time for data processing loops.' AND explanation IS NOT DISTINCT FROM 'LIST_APPEND opcode eliminates attribute lookup and frame stack overhead compared to .append().';
UPDATE question_bank SET question_text='In a GIL-enabled CPython build, how does the Global Interpreter Lock affect multiple threads executing CPU-bound Python bytecode?',explanation='In a GIL-enabled interpreter, only one thread executes Python bytecode at a time. Native extensions may release the GIL, and optional free-threaded CPython builds have different behavior.' WHERE id='bcf1ba10-9bff-4e9b-9946-ba7f452186cf' AND question_text IS NOT DISTINCT FROM 'How does CPython’s Global Interpreter Lock (GIL) impact multithreaded CPU-bound Python programs?' AND explanation IS NOT DISTINCT FROM 'CPython uses the GIL to ensure thread-safe memory management (reference counts). Because only one thread holds the GIL to execute bytecode at any instant, CPU-bound tasks cannot achieve true multi-core parallelism with threading (requiring multiprocessing instead).';
UPDATE question_bank SET question_text='Describe reference counting, cyclic garbage collection and small-object allocation in traditional GIL-enabled CPython, noting implementation-version differences.',correct_answer_text='Reference counting tracks strong references and generally reclaims objects when the count reaches zero. Cyclic garbage collection detects unreachable reference cycles that counting alone cannot reclaim. CPython also uses specialized allocation strategies for small objects. Exact generations, arena sizes, allocator behavior and immortal-object handling depend on version, platform and build, so they should not be presented as universal constants.',explanation='This describes CPython implementation techniques rather than requirements of the Python language. Version and build details matter.' WHERE id='c54e303d-eca7-4e98-8cdf-1eb93893cd99' AND question_text IS NOT DISTINCT FROM 'Describe Python’s memory management model, detailing reference counting, cyclic garbage collection, and memory pooling via PyMalloc.' AND correct_answer_text IS NOT DISTINCT FROM 'CPython employs a multi-tiered memory architecture: 1. Reference Counting: Every Python object header (`PyObject`) stores an `ob_refcnt`. When an object is referenced, its count increments; when a reference goes out of scope or is deleted, it decrements. When `ob_refcnt` hits 0, deallocation is instantaneous. 2. Cyclic Garbage Collector (gc module): Reference counting cannot reclaim cyclic references (e.g. A references B, B references A). CPython uses a generational cyclic collector that partitions heap objects into three generations (G0, G1, G2). It uses a pointer traversal algorithm to isolate and deallocate unreferenced isolated reference cycles. 3. PyMalloc (Arena/Pool/Block allocator): To avoid frequent, fragmented OS malloc calls for small allocations (≤512 bytes), CPython uses PyMalloc to carve 256 KB Arenas into 4 KB Pools consisting of uniform fixed-size memory Blocks.' AND explanation IS NOT DISTINCT FROM 'CPython combines deterministic immediate ref-count destruction with generational cycle detection and small-object arena pooling.';
UPDATE question_bank SET correct_answer_text='RFC 5681 describes slow start, congestion avoidance, fast retransmit and fast recovery. Slow start grows cwnd by at most one SMSS for each acknowledgement of new data, producing roughly exponential growth per RTT under common conditions. The initial window is not universally one segment: RFC 5681 gives bounds of 2, 3 or 4 SMSS depending on SMSS. Congestion avoidance increases cwnd by roughly one SMSS per RTT. Three duplicate ACKs trigger fast retransmit. Reno fast recovery uses ssthresh at no more than max(FlightSize/2, 2*SMSS), temporarily inflates cwnd for duplicate ACKs, and returns cwnd to ssthresh when recovery completes. Later TCP specifications and implementations can differ.',explanation='Initial-window rules differ from the one-segment loss window used after a retransmission timeout. See RFC 5681 sections 3.1 and 3.2.' WHERE id='24cd12c2-5fd1-4044-9b68-2ef53a61f165' AND correct_answer_text IS NOT DISTINCT FROM '1. Slow Start: The sender initializes Congestion Window CWND = 1 MSS. For each incoming non-duplicate ACK, CWND increases by 1 MSS, resulting in exponential doubling of CWND every Round-Trip Time (RTT). This continues until CWND reaches the slow start threshold (ssthresh). 2. Congestion Avoidance: Once CWND ≥ ssthresh, growth shifts from exponential to linear: CWND increases by approximately 1 MSS per RTT (specifically, CWND += MSS × MSS / CWND on each ACK), probing for bandwidth conservatively (AIMD: Additive Increase). 3. Fast Retransmit: When the sender receives 3 duplicate ACKs for the same sequence number, it assumes packet loss has occurred without waiting for a retransmission timeout (RTO). The missing segment is retransmitted immediately. 4. Fast Recovery: Instead of resetting CWND back to 1 MSS (which would trigger Slow Start), ssthresh is set to max(FlightSize/2, 2 MSS) and CWND is set to ssthresh + 3 MSS. For each additional duplicate ACK received, CWND is incremented by 1 MSS. When an ACK for the new unacknowledged data arrives, CWND is set to ssthresh and the algorithm resumes normal Congestion Avoidance.' AND explanation IS NOT DISTINCT FROM 'Standard TCP Reno congestion framework providing graceful AIMD bandwidth convergence and rapid packet loss recovery.';
UPDATE question_bank SET question_text='Name the early embryonic structure consisting of an inner cell mass and an outer trophoblast layer that implants into the uterine endometrium.' WHERE id='462eb1cc-34bf-4935-9af5-8989be7bc66e' AND question_text IS NOT DISTINCT FROM 'Name the female embryonic structure consisting of an inner cell mass and an outer trophoblast layer that implants into the uterine endometrium.';
UPDATE question_bank SET question_text='What is the oxidation number of Oxygen in oxygen difluoride (OF₂)?' WHERE id='00759274-29a8-4236-bd65-da55cfd5d431' AND question_text IS NOT DISTINCT FROM 'What is the oxidation number of Oxygen in fluorine monoxide (OF₂)?';
UPDATE question_bank SET question_text='At what magnitude of displacement from equilibrium does a simple harmonic oscillator have equal kinetic and potential energy, taking potential energy as zero at equilibrium?',explanation='From (1/2)kx² = (1/4)kA², x²=A²/2. The two positions are x=±A/√2, so the magnitude is A/√2.' WHERE id='7e77807c-d8b9-42d3-ba21-e2b511c7eea7' AND question_text IS NOT DISTINCT FROM 'At what displacement from the mean position does the kinetic energy of a simple harmonic oscillator equal its potential energy?' AND explanation IS NOT DISTINCT FROM 'Potential energy U = (1/2)kx² and total energy E = (1/2)kA². Setting U = E/2 gives (1/2)kx² = (1/4)kA² => x² = A²/2 => x = A / √2.';
UPDATE question_bank SET question_text='A body of mass 5 kg is uniformly accelerated across a frictionless surface from rest to 10 m/s in 2 seconds. What is the net applied force?' WHERE id='64138a74-9ec6-4ebd-b00a-70920cfc3c45' AND question_text IS NOT DISTINCT FROM 'A body of mass 5 kg is accelerated across a frictionless surface from rest to 10 m/s in 2 seconds. What is the net applied force?';
UPDATE question_bank SET question_text='Approximately what is the orbital period in hours of a geostationary satellite orbiting Earth? Round to the nearest whole hour.',explanation='A geostationary orbit has a sidereal-day period, approximately 23 hours 56 minutes. This rounds to 24 hours.' WHERE id='448e3505-0ff8-48ad-a8df-e598d836c914' AND question_text IS NOT DISTINCT FROM 'What is the orbital period in hours of a geostationary satellite orbiting Earth?' AND explanation IS NOT DISTINCT FROM 'A geostationary satellite matches Earth’s sidereal rotational period, remaining fixed relative to the ground with an orbital period of approximately 24 hours.';
